## Two-factor authentication

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#80

### Summary
Add optional two-factor authentication (2FA) via an authenticator app (Google Authenticator,
Authy, etc. — any app speaking standard TOTP), using ASP.NET Core Identity's built-in TOTP
support. `AddDefaultIdentity<ApplicationUser>` (`Program.cs`) already registers Identity's
default token providers, so no `Program.cs` change is needed to enable TOTP itself.

### Motivation
Accounts currently authenticate with just a username/password. The Identity scaffolding here
is minimal, so most 2FA pages don't exist yet, though some plumbing already expects them:
`_ManageNav.cshtml` links to a `TwoFactorAuthentication` page (currently 404s), and
`Login.cshtml.cs` already redirects to `./LoginWith2fa` on `RequiresTwoFactor` — a dead path
today. This adds the missing pages.

### Proposed Changes
1. Add the missing scaffolded Identity pages under `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account/Manage/` and
   `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account/`:
   - `TwoFactorAuthentication` (Manage) — shows current 2FA status (enabled/disabled, recovery
     codes remaining) and links to enable/disable/regenerate-codes actions.
   - `EnableAuthenticator` (Manage) — displays the shared key and a QR code, and confirms
     enabling by validating a submitted TOTP code.
   - `GenerateRecoveryCodes` / `ShowRecoveryCodes` (Manage) — regenerates and displays the
     10-code set once; regenerating invalidates the old set.
   - `ResetAuthenticator` (Manage) — disables 2FA and clears the stored authenticator key, so
     re-enabling requires a fresh QR scan.
   - `Disable2fa` (Manage) — disables 2FA without clearing the key.
   - `LoginWith2fa` / `LoginWithRecoveryCode` (Account) — post-password TOTP/recovery-code
     challenge, completing the redirect `Login.cshtml.cs` already issues on `RequiresTwoFactor`.
2. **QR code generation is server-side**, via the `QRCoder` NuGet package: render the
   `otpauth://` URI (built from `UserManager.GetAuthenticatorKeyAsync`) to a PNG/SVG and embed
   it as a `data:` URI directly in `EnableAuthenticator.cshtml` — no client-side JS QR library,
   keeping the shared secret out of any third-party JS's reach and avoiding the site's first
   runtime JS dependency. This is a new NuGet package addition — flagged per CLAUDE.md's
   package-introduction rule; `QRCoder` is a small, widely-used, dependency-free QR renderer
   with no external service calls (fully offline/server-side).
3. Issuer label shown in the authenticator app: `"WhenWorks"` (e.g. `WhenWorks:user@example.com`).
4. **Admin role requires 2FA** (scope addition beyond the original issue text, confirmed with
   the developer): granting the `Admin` role no longer requires 2FA to already be enabled —
   instead, `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]` pages redirect an Admin-role account without 2FA
   enabled to the `EnableAuthenticator` setup page until they complete it. This applies to any
   Admin-role account, including the hardcoded root admin (`kenny@mail.com`) once they choose
   to enable it — the root admin's protection from demotion is unaffected and unrelated.
   - Needs a small enforcement mechanism (e.g. a Razor Page filter/base check in the `Admin`
     area, or a check added to `ManageUsersModel`'s pages) — exact placement to be decided
     during implementation, consistent with "not modifying `Areas/Identity` beyond what's
     already scoped here."
5. Recovery codes: enabling 2FA generates 10 recovery codes, shown once; a lost-device sign-in
   can use one instead (`LoginWithRecoveryCode`).
6. **Close the Google sign-in 2FA bypass.** Google sign-in (#79) is implemented, and
   `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`'s `OnGetCallbackAsync` currently calls
   `_signInManager.ExternalLoginSignInAsync(info.LoginProvider, info.ProviderKey,
   isPersistent: false, bypassTwoFactor: true)` — a 2FA-enabled account signing in via Google
   skips the TOTP challenge entirely today. Change `bypassTwoFactor` to `false` there so a
   returning Google-linked account with 2FA enabled gets redirected through `LoginWith2fa` the
   same as a local password sign-in, instead of silently bypassing it. This is required for the
   Admin-must-have-2FA rule (below) to mean anything for an admin who signs in via Google.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A user can enable 2FA by scanning a server-rendered QR code (or entering the setup key
  manually) and confirming a generated TOTP code.
- Once enabled, sign-in requires a valid TOTP code after a correct password.
- Enabling 2FA generates and displays 10 recovery codes once; regenerating invalidates the old
  set; a recovery code can be used in place of a TOTP code at sign-in.
- The Manage "Two-factor authentication" page shows accurate enabled/disabled status and
  recovery codes remaining, and lets a user disable 2FA.
- Disabling via `ResetAuthenticator` clears the stored key, requiring a fresh QR scan to
  re-enable.
- An Admin-role account without 2FA enabled is redirected to set it up before it can use any
  `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]` page; once enabled, it can use those pages normally.
- A 2FA-enabled account signing in via Google is challenged for a TOTP/recovery code the same
  as a local password sign-in — `bypassTwoFactor` no longer silently skips it.
- No schema changes (`ApplicationUser`/Identity already has the necessary TOTP columns via
  `IdentityUser`'s base schema — verify no migration is actually needed once implementation
  starts, since 2FA fields are part of the standard Identity table set already in place).

### Out of Scope
- SMS-based 2FA (needs phone verification + an SMS provider — its own future issue).
- A "linked login requires re-auth" or per-provider 2FA policy — the fix above is a single
  global flag flip (`bypassTwoFactor: false`), not a configurable per-provider rule.
